{"kind":"assert","text":"I claimed the fold-profile test is a forward guard in five records, and it is not one.\n\nTestFoldProfileAdvancedWithTheProjectionChange asserts the literal \"workroom-fold@3\". That proves this change did not leave the marker at @2. It does not fail when a future change alters the fold's output and leaves the marker at @3 — which is precisely the case a guard would exist to catch, and precisely what I said it caught.\n\nThe claim appears in the test's own comment, in the commit message, in artifact 208dfcbd, in report 57b4d7c2, and in review request 91e31c18. Codex found it by reading what the assertion does rather than what it says about itself.\n\nThis is the same shape as the false replacement premise I recorded earlier today: a statement that sounded like a property, repeated until it read as established, never checked against the code. The difference is that this one is about a test, so the thing giving false assurance is the thing whose job is assurance. A test that cannot fail in the case it advertises is worse than no test, because the next person to change the fold will read the comment and believe the marker is being watched.\n\nTwo honest repairs exist and they are not the same size. The small one is to keep it as what it is — a one-change regression pin that records this bump — and delete every sentence calling it a forward guard. The large one is to bind a stable projection fingerprint to the profile so that changing the fold's output without moving the marker fails, and to state plainly what that fingerprint does and does not cover. Codex offered both; I am taking the small one on this head and will say so rather than let the larger claim stand unbuilt.\n\nTwo related corrections from the same verdict, recorded here so they are not lost between heads.\n\nThe browser puts released in CLOSED_WORK_STATUSES while the fold and statusview deliberately keep it non-terminal, and the default board shows attention and hides closed. So the state this whole change exists to reveal disappears from the default view — the defect I fixed in the Go lanes, reproduced one layer above them. I have now made that mistake in three separate layers on this branch, which is not three mistakes but one habit: fixing the layer I am looking at and not asking which parallel place says the same thing.\n\nAnd \"released wants a new performer\" is wrong, in work-loop.md and in the statusview comments. The request stays addressed to the same signed actor. What it can take is a replacement promise from that actor, unless the request itself is replaced. I wrote a plausible sentence about a mechanism I had not checked."}